  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1148 PM CDT Sun May 24 2026\n\nAn upper level ridge will slowly slide eastward through tomorrow \nwith an upper level low digging into the Pacific Northwest. Lee \ntroughing will allow breezy southelry winds to be felt across \nwestern Kansas with some high clouds present through tomorrow \nmorning. A few afternoon thunderstorms will form across eastern \nColorado this afternoon but I believe that these storms should stay \nout of our CWA. Highs today will reach into the upper 80s with some \nplaces reaching around 90 degrees. Lows tonight will dip into the \nmid 50s to around 60 degrees with highs tomorrow in the 80s. \n\nThe aforementioned upper level low will continue to dig into the \nwestern Unites states and push the upper level ridge east of the \narea. Pieces of energy will wrap around the periphery of this \nfeature and eject into the central Plains. This will bring an \nincrease in precipitation across the CWA. Precipitation chances \nincrease starting Tuesday night with the best chance of \nprecipitation Wednesday afternoon into Thursday morning. \nPrecipitation chances are then confined across central Kansas \nThursday afternoon into Friday. Additional disturbances move through \nthe area this weekend bringing another chance of precipitation. \nFuture model runs will need to be watched to see what comes to \nfruition. Otherwise, expect an increase in cloudiness as these \ndisturbances move through. As of now, SPC and AI models do not have \nvery high probabilities of these storms to be severe. As for \ntemperatures, highs will reach into the mid 70s to mid 80s Wednesday \nand Thursday with 80s to around 90 degrees for the remainder of the \nforecast. Lows will range from the upper 40s to mid 60s.",
